WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.500
Stop mowing a field in the Virginia Piedmont.

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:08.520
Nobody plants anything.

3
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.360
Seeds blow in or wait in the soil.

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.440
Within a few years, the field can fill with broomsedge, blackberry and small

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.300
pines. After a century or more, oaks and hickories can take over.

6
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.960
What happens to a field after people or fire leave it alone?

7
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.720
This change has a name: ecological succession.

8
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.380
A community is all the populations of different species living in one area.

9
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.500
Succession is the change, over years to centuries, in which species live there.

10
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.920
The change is predictable in a general way.

11
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.360
Certain plants tend to come early, others late.

12
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.270
Succession often starts after a disturbance.

13
00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:01.090
A disturbance is an event, like a fire, flood or plowing, that removes much of a

14
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:05.270
community. If the soil stays, it is secondary succession.

15
00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.250
On bare rock there is no soil.

16
00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:13.350
That is primary succession, and lichens and mosses usually arrive first.

17
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.750
Secondary succession usually reaches forest much faster.

18
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:24.403
Here is the usual path published for old Piedmont fields, just south of Virginia.

19
00:01:25.423 --> 00:01:30.823
In the first years, weeds such as horseweed and asters grow fast in full sun.

20
00:01:31.703 --> 00:01:36.563
By about the third year, broomsedge, a tall grass, often covers the field.

21
00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:39.363
Pines grow fast in full sun.

22
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:43.663
Within a few decades, the field becomes a young pine forest.

23
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:47.953
Each stage changes the place it grows in.

24
00:01:48.713 --> 00:01:53.853
Abiotic factors are the nonliving parts of an ecosystem, such as light and soil.

25
00:01:54.853 --> 00:01:57.933
A closed pine canopy blocks much of the sunlight.

26
00:01:58.953 --> 00:02:01.173
Pine seedlings grow poorly in that shade.

27
00:02:02.213 --> 00:02:06.033
Young hardwoods, like oaks and hickories, cope with shade better.

28
00:02:06.773 --> 00:02:10.053
As the old pines die, the hardwoods fill the gaps.

29
00:02:10.967 --> 00:02:14.227
So why do plants arrive in this order?

30
00:02:15.107 --> 00:02:20.307
Each stage changes light and soil, and those changes favor the next plants.

31
00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:26.467
In most of Virginia, the late stage is an oak-hickory hardwood forest, the climax

32
00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:33.227
community. But fire, storms and people reset patches, so the path and timing vary.

33
00:02:33.227 --> 00:02:38.247
Each stage changes the place, and that favors the next plants.
